 We set out to quantify how INT8 quantization changes model explanations, and found first that the standard way of extracting those explanations was itself the largest source of apparent change. The widely used ``last convolutional layer'' heuristic returns a globally pooled $1 \times 1$ tensor for all three architectures studied, forcing a constant saliency map and manufacturing attribution collapse in up to $61.7\%$ of images independently of precision. With target layers resolved by spatial extent, no collapse occurs in any of $3{,}240$ paired comparisons.
 
-On that corrected footing, quantization does perturb explanations, measurably and architecture-dependently, but bounded well above chance: top-$k$ IoU $0.366$--$0.790$ against a random-overlap baseline of $0.081$, median Spearman $\rho = 0.850$. Two interventions were tested against controls. A CAM-consistency QAT objective helps MobileNetV3-Large ($+16.3\%$ top-$k$ IoU) but harms EfficientNetV2-S (up to $-26.0\%$) relative to post-training quantization; benchmarking against a $\lambda = 0$ arm attributes that split to quantization-aware training rather than to the consistency term, which improves attribution preservation in all nine architecture--method cells against its own control. These results motivate architecture-specific evaluation of QAT and classification trade-offs. Retaining the CAM target stage in FP32 raises MobileNetV3-Large top-$k$ IoU from $0.582$ to $0.841$ without retraining in a simulation diagnostic; deployed efficiency and parameter overhead remain unmeasured.
+On that corrected footing, quantization does perturb explanations, measurably and architecture-dependently, with architecture--method mean overlaps well above the random-overlap baseline in the simulation: top-$k$ IoU $0.366$--$0.790$ against a random-overlap baseline of $0.081$, median Spearman $\rho = 0.850$. Two interventions were tested against controls. A CAM-consistency QAT objective helps MobileNetV3-Large ($+16.3\%$ top-$k$ IoU) but harms EfficientNetV2-S (up to $-26.0\%$) relative to post-training quantization; the seed-42 $\lambda = 0$ control shows that this directional split is already present without the consistency term, which improves attribution preservation in all nine architecture--method cells against its own control. These results motivate architecture-specific evaluation of QAT and classification trade-offs. Retaining the CAM target stage in FP32 raises MobileNetV3-Large top-$k$ IoU from $0.582$ to $0.841$ without retraining in a simulation diagnostic; deployed efficiency and parameter overhead remain unmeasured.
 
 We also report two findings that constrain how any of this should be used. Calibration choice dominates: on the full validation split the default MinMax calibrator reduces deployed FP32--INT8 agreement to $0.641$--$0.911$, whereas percentile calibration achieves $0.915$--$0.971$ without changing bit width. And explanation similarity is not explanation quality: the auxiliary expert-mask test shows low overlap but does not establish whether either precision exceeds chance, so saliency similarity describes preservation of a reference map rather than certification of model behaviour or explanation quality. Auditing a quantized deployment requires verified attribution targets, an explicit null model, a reported calibration protocol, and a faithfulness test alongside any similarity metric.
 
